@ifnottex
@node Top
@top Capsule

Capsule runs one program fullscreen on a seat and exits with that program's
exit status.  It is a Wayland kiosk compositor in Rust, the Spacecraft
Software successor to Cage.

This edition carries the needs and the software requirements specification
for version @value{VERSION}, at assurance Category B (The Steelbore Standard
§19).  Status: draft, awaiting gate G1.

@insertcopying
@end ifnottex

@menu
* Terminology::         Terms used with one meaning throughout.
* Needs::               Why Capsule exists.
* Requirements::        What Capsule shall do.
@end menu

@node Terminology
@chapter Terminology

@table @dfn
@item program
The executable named on Capsule's command line, after Capsule's own options.
@item child
The process Capsule starts to run the program.
@item client
Any process connected to Capsule's Wayland socket.  The child is usually the
first client; programs it starts may be others.
@item toplevel
A window: an @code{xdg_toplevel} under the Wayland backends, or a top-level
window under the X11 kiosk mode.
@item parent
The toplevel another toplevel is attached to: set through
@code{xdg_toplevel.set_parent} under Wayland, or through the
@code{WM_TRANSIENT_FOR} property under X11.  A toplevel with a parent is a
@dfn{dialog}.
@item internal panel
An output on an @samp{eDP}, @samp{LVDS}, or @samp{DSI} connector.
@item introspection flag
One of @option{--version}, @option{--help}, @option{--describe},
@option{--schema}, and @option{--dry-run}.
@item configuration directory
@env{XDG_CONFIG_HOME} (or @file{~/.config} when it is unset) and every
directory named in @env{XDG_CONFIG_DIRS} (or @file{/etc/xdg} when it is
unset).
@item output
A display Capsule can show its toplevels on.
@item connector name
The kernel's name for a display connector, such as @samp{eDP-1} or
@samp{HDMI-A-1}: the part after @samp{card@var{n}-} in the connector's
@file{/sys/class/drm} entry.  Connector names are ordered by byte value.
@item lid closed
The state reported by a libinput lid-switch device while the lid is shut.
@item seat session
Capsule's access to a seat's display and input devices, granted by the seat
manager (seatd or systemd-logind) through libseat.
@item DRM backend
The mode in which Capsule drives a display directly through kernel mode
setting and reads input devices through libinput.
@item nested backend
The mode in which Capsule shows its output as one window of a running Wayland
or X11 session and opens no device of its own.
@item X11 kiosk mode
The optional mode in which Capsule is the window manager of an X server
instead of a Wayland compositor.
@item active
The seat session is @dfn{active} while its virtual terminal is the one in
the foreground.
@item CLI Standard
The Spacecraft Software Dual-Mode Self-Documenting CLI Standard, version
1.1.0.  Every citation of it in this manual means that version.
@item machine mode
The output mode in which Capsule writes diagnostics as JSON, selected by the
CLI Standard §5 cascade.
@item evidence marker
A comment @samp{Verifies: CPS-SRS-@var{nnn}} on a test, or
@samp{Implements: CPS-SRS-@var{nnn}} on the item that implements a
requirement.
@item parser
Code in Capsule's own crates that turns bytes or text from outside the
process into structured values: the command line, environment variables,
and the sysfs and udev attributes read to choose a GPU and an output.
@item agent environment
A process environment in which @env{AI_AGENT} or @env{AGENT} is set to a
non-empty value, or @env{CI} is set to a value other than @samp{0} or
@samp{false}.
@item canvas colour
The canvas token of the palette resolved under The Steelbore Standard §11.6.
@item reference machine
The maintainer's ThinkPad (Intel Core i7-8665U, 4 cores and 8 threads, Intel
UHD Graphics 620, NixOS as Steelbore OS Bravais).  Memory and storage class
are recorded at gate G2 under §22.4.
@item reference workload
Alacritty running Nushell under the DRM backend, printing a committed
10 MiB text file in a loop for 600 s, then idle for 60 s.
@end table

@node Needs
@chapter Needs

@table @asis
@item CPS-NEED-001 @emph{One program, whole screen}
A greeter session runs exactly one program, a terminal running a shell, on
the whole screen, with no desktop, panel, or other window manager around it.

@item CPS-NEED-002 @emph{Ends with its program}
When that program exits, the session ends, the greeter returns, and the
session reports how the program ended.

@item CPS-NEED-003 @emph{Never stranded}
A crash, a misbehaving client, or a missing device never leaves the seat
without a working display and keyboard.  The user can always switch to
another virtual terminal and back, and from another virtual terminal can
always end the session and get back to the greeter.

@item CPS-NEED-004 @emph{Works on the maintainer's hardware}
The session works on the Bravais machines, including a machine without a
working OpenGL ES driver, across monitor plugging and unplugging and across
suspend and resume.

@item CPS-NEED-005 @emph{Independent and small}
The session depends on no desktop environment.  No desktop's shortcuts or
configuration leak into it, and rebuilding a desktop never rebuilds or
changes the login path.

@item CPS-NEED-006 @emph{Private by default}
No Wayland client in the session can capture the screen or inject input
into another program through the compositor.  Under the optional XWayland
and X11 kiosk modes, X11 clients can do both through the X server; that
limit is accepted for those modes.

@item CPS-NEED-007 @emph{Operable by people and agents}
Capsule is started, inspected, and diagnosed from the command line by a
person at a terminal or by an AI agent, under the Spacecraft Software CLI
Standard.

@item CPS-NEED-008 @emph{Assistive technology first}
Capsule never takes a key away from a screen reader, and never stands
between the program and the accessibility bus.

@item CPS-NEED-009 @emph{Developed without a spare seat}
Capsule can be run and tested inside the maintainer's existing desktop
session, under Wayland or X11, without logging out.

@item CPS-NEED-010 @emph{Machines without kernel mode setting (optional)}
On a machine where only an X server can drive the display, Capsule started
with @option{--backend x11} as the @command{xinit} client gives the same
one-program session.
@end table

Constraints the maintainer will not trade away: one program at a time, never
a desktop (no tiling, workspaces, panels, or configuration file); the
language is Rust; the platform is Linux; the command line stays
Cage-compatible in shape (@code{capsule <program> <args>}).

@node Requirements
@chapter Requirements

Every requirement uses the §20.6 sentence pattern and carries the five
§20.3 attributes.  Methods are the four of §21.1: test, analysis,
inspection, and review of design.  Budgets in @ref{Budgets} are provisional
until gate G2.

@strong{Scope.} Each section opens by stating the backends its requirements
apply under.  A requirement whose own text names a backend or mode applies
under that backend or mode alone.

@menu
* Foundation::                  CPS-SRS-001 to 009.
* Command line::                CPS-SRS-010 to 031, 106 to 108, 110, 111, 121.
* Session and protocols::       CPS-SRS-032 to 053, 109, 113, 117, 122 to 126.
* DRM backend::                 CPS-SRS-054 to 072, 116, 118, 119.
* Nested backend::              CPS-SRS-073 to 076, 115.
* Keys and accessibility::      CPS-SRS-077 to 078.
* Budgets::                     CPS-SRS-079 to 083.
* Release::                     CPS-SRS-084 to 093, 127.
* XWayland::                    CPS-SRS-094 to 097, optional.
* X11 kiosk mode::              CPS-SRS-098 to 105, 112, 114, 120, optional.
@end menu

@node Foundation
@section Foundation

@table @asis
@item CPS-SRS-001 @emph{REUSE}
The repository shall pass @command{reuse lint} with exit status 0.

Rationale: every file needs a machine-readable licence. Source: Standard
§4.3. Method: inspection. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-002 @emph{Posture files}
The repository shall carry @file{README.md}, @file{NOTICE.md},
@file{CONTRIBUTING.md}, @file{LICENSE}, and @file{LICENSES/} at its root.

Rationale: the project posture is stated where a reader looks first.
Source: Standard §5.2. Method: inspection. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-003 @emph{Agent context files}
The repository shall carry @file{AGENTS.md}, @file{CLAUDE.md}, and
@file{SKILL.md} at its root.

Rationale: agents load project invariants and the command surface from these
files. Source: Standard §5.7; CLI Standard rule 4. Method: inspection.
Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-004 @emph{Category declared}
@file{README.md}, @file{AGENTS.md}, and the @file{PROJECTS.md} registry
shall each declare assurance Category B.

Rationale: the category decides the evidence owed. Source: Standard §19.2.
Method: inspection. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-005 @emph{Security policy}
The repository shall carry a @file{SECURITY.md} naming the reporting
channel, acknowledgement target, scope, supported versions, disclosure
terms, and credit policy.

Rationale: Capsule holds every input device of a seat, so a reporting path is
needed. Source: Standard §26.1. Method: inspection. Priority: mandatory.
Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-006 @emph{Tailoring register}
The repository shall carry a @file{COMPLIANCE.md} holding the §19.5
tailoring register.

Rationale: Category B records tailoring in a dedicated file. Source: Standard
§19.5. Method: inspection. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-007 @emph{CI gate}
The CI pipeline shall block a merge unless @command{cargo fmt --check},
@command{cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features --locked -- -D warnings},
@command{cargo test}, @command{cargo audit}, and @command{cargo deny check}
each exit with status 0.

Rationale: defects and vulnerable dependencies are caught before merge.
Source: Standard §3.3, §24.2. Method: inspection. Priority: mandatory.
Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-008 @emph{Text format}
The CI pipeline shall fail when a tracked text file contains a carriage
return byte.

Rationale: line endings stay LF across contributors. Source: Standard §6.5.
Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-009 @emph{Traceability gate}
The CI pipeline shall fail when a baselined requirement has no evidence
marker or a marker cites an identifier absent from the requirement set.

Rationale: work specified and never done, and evidence citing a withdrawn
requirement, are both caught mechanically. Source: Standard §21.3. Method:
test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.
@end table

@node Command line
@section Command line

These requirements apply under every backend and mode.

@table @asis
@item CPS-SRS-010 @emph{Start the program}
When the command line names a program, Capsule shall start that program as
its child once it can show the child's toplevels: under the Wayland backends
once its Wayland socket is listening, and in X11 kiosk mode once it manages
the display.

Rationale: running one program is the whole purpose. Source: CPS-NEED-001.
Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-011 @emph{Child arguments}
Capsule shall pass every argument that follows the program name to the
child unchanged and in order.

Rationale: a child option must never be read as a Capsule option.
Source: CPS-NEED-001. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-012 @emph{End of options}
Capsule shall treat the argument @code{--} as the end of its own options.

Rationale: a program whose name begins with @samp{-} stays runnable.
Source: POSIX utility syntax guideline 10. Method: test. Priority:
mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-013 @emph{Missing program}
When the command line names neither a program nor an introspection flag,
Capsule shall exit with status 2 after writing an error whose hint is a
runnable example invocation.

Rationale: a usage error is reported, never guessed around. Source: CLI
Standard §4; CPS-NEED-007. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-014 @emph{Exit status}
When the child exits normally, Capsule shall exit with the child's exit
status, except as CPS-SRS-117 provides.

Rationale: the greeter reports how the session's program ended.
Source: CPS-NEED-002. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-015 @emph{Signal exit}
When the child is terminated by signal @var{n}, Capsule shall exit with
status 128 + @var{n}, except as CPS-SRS-117 provides.

Rationale: the shell convention lets a caller tell a crash from an exit.
Source: CPS-NEED-002; CLI Standard §4. Method: test. Priority: mandatory.
Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-016 @emph{Program not found}
When the program cannot be found, Capsule shall exit with status 127.

Rationale: the POSIX-reserved code for a missing command. Source: CLI
Standard §4. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-017 @emph{Program not executable}
When the program is found but cannot be executed, Capsule shall exit with
status 126.

Rationale: the POSIX-reserved code for a command that cannot run. Source:
CLI Standard §4. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-018 @emph{Global flags}
Capsule shall accept every global flag of the CLI Standard §3, with the
argument that section defines, without a usage error.

Rationale: every Spacecraft Software CLI accepts the same flags; the flags
whose effect matters to Capsule carry their own requirements. Source: CLI
Standard §3. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-019 @emph{Describe}
When given @option{--describe}, Capsule shall write its capability manifest,
a JSON document whose schema the @code{Interfaces} node records, to standard
output and exit with status 0 without opening a display or a seat session.

Rationale: an agent learns the tool from the tool. Source: CLI Standard
rule 4; CPS-NEED-007. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-020 @emph{Schema}
When given @option{--schema}, Capsule shall write a JSON Schema Draft
2020-12 document describing its command line to standard output and exit
with status 0 without opening a display or a seat session.

Rationale: the schema drops straight into a function-calling API. Source:
CLI Standard rule 4; CPS-NEED-007. Method: test. Priority: mandatory.
Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-021 @emph{Dry run}
When given @option{--dry-run}, Capsule shall write the backend, GPU, and
output it would select, as a JSON document whose schema the
@code{Interfaces} node records, to standard output and exit with status 0
without starting the child or opening a seat session.  Under the nested
backend the output is the host window and no GPU is reported.

Rationale: the plan can be checked without taking the seat. Source: CLI
Standard §3; CPS-NEED-007. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-022 @emph{Version}
When given @option{--version}, Capsule shall write its version, the line
@samp{Maintained by Mohamed Hammad <Mohamed.Hammad@@SpacecraftSoftware.org>},
and the project URL to standard output, and exit with status 0.

Rationale: attribution in every output surface. Source: Standard §15; CLI
Standard §3. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-023 @emph{Help}
When given @option{--help}, Capsule shall write at least two example
invocations and a footer naming the project URL and the maintainer to
standard output, and exit with status 0.

Rationale: help teaches by example. Source: CLI Standard §3. Method: test.
Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-024 @emph{Structured errors}
In machine mode, when Capsule itself fails, it shall write one JSON error
object to standard error carrying @code{code}, @code{exit_code},
@code{message}, @code{hint}, @code{timestamp}, @code{command}, and
@code{docs_url}.

Rationale: an agent acts on fields, not prose. Source: CLI Standard §1
rule 8; CPS-NEED-007. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-025 @emph{Severity tags}
Outside machine mode, Capsule shall begin every diagnostic line on standard
error with @samp{[ERROR]}, @samp{[WARN]}, @samp{[OK]}, or @samp{[INFO]}.

Rationale: severity is never carried by colour alone. Source: CLI Standard
§1 rule 8; Standard §18.2.1. Method: test. Priority: mandatory.
Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-026 @emph{Agent environment}
In an agent environment, Capsule shall select machine mode unless an
explicit format flag selects another.

Rationale: agents get JSON without having to ask. Source: CLI Standard §5.
Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-027 @emph{Standard output}
Capsule shall not write to standard output except in answer to
@option{--version}, @option{--help}, @option{--describe},
@option{--schema}, or @option{--dry-run}.

Rationale: the child inherits standard output, and Capsule must not corrupt
it. Source: CLI Standard §1 rule 7. Method: test. Priority: mandatory.
Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-028 @emph{Timestamps}
Capsule shall write every timestamp as ISO 8601 in UTC with the @samp{Z}
suffix.

Rationale: one unambiguous time format in logs and JSON. Source: Standard
§14. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-029 @emph{Backend flag}
Capsule shall accept @option{--backend} with the values @samp{auto},
@samp{drm}, @samp{nested}, and @samp{x11}, defaulting to @samp{auto}.

Rationale: the operator can force a backend when detection is wrong, and
the optional X11 kiosk mode is reached only by name (CPS-SRS-108 covers a
build without it). Source: CPS-NEED-009; CPS-NEED-010. Method: test. Priority: mandatory.
Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-030 @emph{Automatic backend}
When the backend is @samp{auto}, Capsule shall select the nested backend if
@env{WAYLAND_DISPLAY} or @env{DISPLAY} is set to a non-empty value, and the
DRM backend otherwise.

Rationale: the same command works in a greeter and inside a desktop.
Source: CPS-NEED-009. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-031 @emph{Verbose report}
When given @option{--verbose}, Capsule shall report the selected backend,
renderer, output name, and output mode as @samp{[INFO]} diagnostics.

Rationale: a wrong choice is visible without a debugger. Source:
CPS-NEED-007. Method: test. Priority: expected. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-106 @emph{Quiet and verbose together}
When given both @option{--quiet} and @option{--verbose}, Capsule shall exit
with status 2.

Rationale: the two flags set opposite severity floors. Source: CLI Standard
§3. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-107 @emph{Quiet}
When given @option{--quiet}, Capsule shall write no diagnostic below
@samp{error} severity.

Rationale: a caller can silence everything but failures. Source: CLI
Standard §3. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-108 @emph{Backend not built}
When @option{--backend} names a backend not built into the running binary,
Capsule shall exit with status 2 after writing an error whose hint names the
backends that are built in.

Rationale: an optional backend left out of a build is reported, never
silently replaced. Source: CLI Standard §4; CPS-NEED-007. Method: test.
Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-110 @emph{No configuration}
The Capsule process shall open no file in a configuration directory, through
Capsule's own code or through any library it loads.

Rationale: no desktop's configuration or shortcuts reach the session.
Source: CPS-NEED-005. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-111 @emph{Version as JSON}
When given @option{--version} in machine mode, Capsule shall write a JSON
envelope whose metadata carries the keys @code{maintainer} and
@code{website}.

Rationale: attribution survives in structured output. Source: CLI Standard
§3; Standard §15. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-121 @emph{XWayland not built}
When given @option{--xwayland} by a binary built without the @code{xwayland}
feature, Capsule shall exit with status 2 after writing an error whose hint
names the feature.

Rationale: a request for an X server that cannot start is reported, never
ignored. Source: CLI Standard §4; CPS-NEED-007. Method: test. Priority:
mandatory. Status: draft.
@end table

@node Session and protocols
@section Session and protocols

These requirements apply under the DRM and nested backends.

@table @asis
@item CPS-SRS-032 @emph{Socket}
Capsule shall create its listening Wayland socket in the directory named by
@env{XDG_RUNTIME_DIR} before it starts the child.

Rationale: the child connects on its first attempt. Source: CPS-NEED-001.
Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-033 @emph{Missing runtime directory}
When @env{XDG_RUNTIME_DIR} is unset, does not name a directory, or names a
directory in which Capsule cannot create its socket, Capsule shall exit with
status 1 after writing an error whose hint names the variable.

Rationale: a broken login environment is reported, not worked around.
Source: CPS-NEED-007. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-034 @emph{Child environment}
Capsule shall start the child with Capsule's own environment plus
@env{WAYLAND_DISPLAY} naming Capsule's socket, and with no other variable
added, changed, or removed except as CPS-SRS-095 and CPS-SRS-113 provide.

Rationale: the session bus and the accessibility bus reach the program
untouched. Source: CPS-NEED-008. Method: test. Priority: mandatory.
Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-035 @emph{Advertised globals}
Capsule shall advertise exactly these globals: @code{wl_compositor},
@code{wl_subcompositor}, @code{wl_shm}, @code{wl_data_device_manager},
@code{wl_seat}, @code{wl_output}, @code{xdg_wm_base},
@code{zxdg_output_manager_v1}, @code{zwp_linux_dmabuf_v1},
@code{wp_viewporter}, @code{wp_presentation},
@code{zwp_primary_selection_device_manager_v1},
@code{zxdg_decoration_manager_v1}, and @code{wp_cursor_shape_manager_v1},
together with @code{xwayland_shell_v1} when @option{--xwayland} is given.

Rationale: no screen-capture, input-injection, or layer-shell global exists
for a client to abuse. Source: CPS-NEED-006. Method: test. Priority:
mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-036 @emph{Toplevels fullscreen}
Capsule shall configure every toplevel that has no parent as fullscreen at
the output's logical size.

Rationale: the program's windows fill the screen, whichever it maps first.
Source: CPS-NEED-001. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-037 @emph{Dialogs centred}
Capsule shall show every dialog at its own requested size, reduced in each
dimension to the output's logical size where it is larger, centred on the
output.

Rationale: dialogs stay visible and usable without a window manager.
Source: CPS-NEED-001. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-038 @emph{Focus newest}
Capsule shall give keyboard focus to the most recently mapped toplevel.

Rationale: a dialog takes the keys as soon as it appears. Source:
CPS-NEED-001. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-039 @emph{Focus return}
When the focused toplevel unmaps, Capsule shall give keyboard focus to the
most recently mapped toplevel still mapped.

Rationale: closing a dialog returns the keys to the program. Source:
CPS-NEED-001. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-040 @emph{Popups}
Capsule shall place each popup where its positioner asks, moved or flipped
as the positioner allows so that it lies inside the output.

Rationale: menus never open off screen. Source: CPS-NEED-001. Method: test.
Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-041 @emph{Keymap}
Capsule shall build its keymap from @env{XKB_DEFAULT_RULES},
@env{XKB_DEFAULT_MODEL}, @env{XKB_DEFAULT_LAYOUT},
@env{XKB_DEFAULT_VARIANT}, and @env{XKB_DEFAULT_OPTIONS}.

Rationale: the layout follows the login environment without a
configuration file. Source: CPS-NEED-001; CPS-NEED-005. Method: test.
Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-042 @emph{Keymap fallback}
When those variables name a keymap that cannot be compiled, Capsule shall
use the keymap of rules @samp{evdev}, model @samp{pc105}, and layout
@samp{us}, and write a @samp{[WARN]} diagnostic naming the variables.

Rationale: a typo in a layout never costs the keyboard. Source:
CPS-NEED-003. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-043 @emph{Pointer focus}
Capsule shall deliver pointer events to the surface under the cursor.

Rationale: the pointer works as the user expects. Source: CPS-NEED-001.
Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-044 @emph{Cursor}
When no surface is under the cursor, or the client under it has set no
cursor, Capsule shall draw the default cursor of the theme named by @env{XCURSOR_THEME} (@samp{default}
when unset) at the size named by @env{XCURSOR_SIZE} (24 when unset).

Rationale: the pointer is never invisible. Source: CPS-NEED-001. Method:
test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-045 @emph{Clipboard}
Capsule shall transfer the clipboard selection between clients.

Rationale: copy and paste work in the terminal and its dialogs. Source:
CPS-NEED-001. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-046 @emph{Primary selection}
Capsule shall transfer the primary selection between clients.

Rationale: select-to-copy and middle-click paste work in the terminal.
Source: CPS-NEED-001. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-047 @emph{No decorations}
Capsule shall answer every decoration request with server-side mode and draw
no decoration.

Rationale: no title bar or border competes with the program. Source:
CPS-NEED-001. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-048 @emph{Background}
Capsule shall paint every part of the output not covered by a surface in the
canvas colour.

Rationale: the visible background follows the house palette. Source:
Standard §11. Method: test. Priority: expected. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-049 @emph{Protocol errors}
When a client causes a protocol error, Capsule shall disconnect that client
and keep serving every other client.

Rationale: a broken client never takes the session down. Source:
CPS-NEED-003. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-050 @emph{Lifetime}
Capsule shall keep running while the child runs, whether or not any client
is connected.

Rationale: a shell between windows is still a session. Source: CPS-NEED-002.
Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-051 @emph{Termination}
When Capsule receives @code{SIGTERM}, @code{SIGINT}, or @code{SIGHUP}, it
shall send @code{SIGTERM} to the child.

Rationale: the greeter can end a session cleanly. Source: CPS-NEED-002.
Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-052 @emph{Termination deadline}
When the child has not exited 5 s after Capsule sent it @code{SIGTERM},
Capsule shall send it @code{SIGKILL}.

Rationale: a hung program cannot hold the seat forever. Source:
CPS-NEED-003. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-053 @emph{Idle}
When no surface has new damage and no client awaits a frame callback,
Capsule shall submit no frame.

Rationale: an idle session costs no power. Source: CPS-NEED-004. Method:
test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-109 @emph{Stacking}
Capsule shall stack the most recently mapped toplevel above every other
toplevel.

Rationale: a newly opened dialog is never hidden behind the program.
Source: CPS-NEED-001. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-113 @emph{Host display removed}
Under the nested backend without @option{--xwayland}, Capsule shall remove
@env{DISPLAY} from the child's environment.

Rationale: an X11 fallback in the program never reaches the host desktop.
Source: CPS-NEED-005; CPS-NEED-006. Method: test. Priority: mandatory.
Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-117 @emph{Fatal error}
When Capsule meets an error it cannot recover from while the child runs, it
shall send @code{SIGTERM} to the child, apply CPS-SRS-052, and exit with
status 1 once the child has exited.

Rationale: a failing compositor ends the session cleanly instead of
orphaning the program. Source: CPS-NEED-003. Method: test. Priority:
mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-122 @emph{Scale}
Capsule shall give the output a scale of 1.

Rationale: one reading of logical size; the program sizes its own text.
Source: CPS-NEED-001. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-123 @emph{Transform}
Capsule shall give the output the normal transform.

Rationale: one reading of logical size and orientation. Source:
CPS-NEED-001. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-124 @emph{Unconstrained popup}
When no adjustment the positioner allows keeps a popup inside the output,
Capsule shall place the popup where the positioner asks, unadjusted.

Rationale: the protocol's own rule for a popup that cannot be constrained.
Source: CPS-NEED-001. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-125 @emph{Built-in cursor}
When no cursor theme can be loaded, Capsule shall draw a cursor image built
into the binary.

Rationale: a bare greeter environment may have no cursor theme installed.
Source: CPS-NEED-003. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-126 @emph{One output}
Capsule shall show toplevels on one output at a time.

Rationale: a kiosk session has one screen; mirroring and spanning are out of
scope. Source: CPS-NEED-001. Method: test. Priority: mandatory.
Status: draft.
@end table

@node DRM backend
@section DRM backend

These requirements apply under the DRM backend.

@table @asis
@item CPS-SRS-054 @emph{Seat session}
Under the DRM backend, Capsule shall open display and input device nodes
only through a seat session.

Rationale: no root privilege and no group membership grants device access.
Source: CPS-NEED-003; Standard §3.3. Method: review of design. Priority:
mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-055 @emph{No seat}
When no seat session can be opened, Capsule shall exit with status 4 after
writing an error whose hint names the seat manager to check.

Rationale: a permission failure is reported as one. Source: CLI Standard
§4. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-056 @emph{GPU choice}
Capsule shall use the DRM card whose PCI device has the sysfs attribute
@file{boot_vga} set to 1, or the card with the lowest minor number when no
device has it.

Rationale: the choice is the one the firmware made, and is predictable.
Source: CPS-NEED-004. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-057 @emph{Internal panel first}
When an internal panel on the chosen GPU is connected, and either the lid is
not closed or no other output on that GPU is connected, Capsule shall use
the connected internal panel with the lowest connector name as the output.

Rationale: a laptop shows the session on its own screen, but not on a shut
lid beside a live monitor. Source:
CPS-NEED-004. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-058 @emph{Output fallback}
When CPS-SRS-057 selects no output, Capsule shall use the connected output on
the chosen GPU with the lowest connector name.

Rationale: the choice is deterministic. Source: CPS-NEED-004. Method: test.
Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-059 @emph{Mode}
Capsule shall drive the output at its preferred mode, or at the first mode
it lists when none is marked preferred.

Rationale: the panel's native resolution and refresh. Source: CPS-NEED-004.
Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-060 @emph{Hardware rendering}
When OpenGL ES initialises on the GPU, Capsule shall composite with it.

Rationale: hardware composition at the panel's refresh rate. Source:
CPS-NEED-004. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-061 @emph{Software rendering}
When OpenGL ES fails to initialise, Capsule shall composite in software and
write a @samp{[WARN]} diagnostic naming the failure.

Rationale: a machine without a working driver still gets a session.
Source: CPS-NEED-004. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-062 @emph{No renderer}
When neither renderer initialises, Capsule shall exit with status 1 after
writing an error naming both failures.

Rationale: an unusable display is reported, and the greeter returns.
Source: CPS-NEED-003. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-063 @emph{Input devices}
Capsule shall deliver input from every keyboard, pointer, and touchpad on
the seat.

Rationale: every attached device works. Source: CPS-NEED-001. Method: test.
Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-064 @emph{Device defaults}
Capsule shall leave every input device at the libinput default
configuration.

Rationale: behaviour such as pointing-stick middle-button scrolling matches
every other session. Source: CPS-NEED-004; Bravais CONSTRAINTS #29.
Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-065 @emph{Virtual terminal switch}
When Ctrl+Alt+F@var{n} is pressed for @var{n} from 1 to 12, Capsule shall
switch to virtual terminal @var{n}.

Rationale: the user can always reach another terminal. Source:
CPS-NEED-003. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-066 @emph{Inactive}
While the seat session is not active, Capsule shall deliver no input to
clients and submit no frame.

Rationale: keys typed on another terminal never reach the program. Source:
CPS-NEED-003; CPS-NEED-006. Method: test. Priority: mandatory.
Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-067 @emph{Reactivation}
When the seat session becomes active again, Capsule shall present a frame on
the output within 1 s.

Rationale: switching back shows the session at once. Source: CPS-NEED-003.
Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-068 @emph{Output re-chosen}
When an output is connected or disconnected, or the lid opens or closes,
Capsule shall apply CPS-SRS-057 and CPS-SRS-058 again and move the session to
the output they select within 2 s.

Rationale: plugging, unplugging, and closing the lid never end the session
or leave it on a dark screen. Source: CPS-NEED-004. Method: test. Priority:
mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-069 @emph{No output}
While no output is connected, Capsule shall keep the child running and shall
resume on the first output connected.

Rationale: a closed lid with no monitor attached never ends the session.
Source: CPS-NEED-004. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-070 @emph{Suspend}
After the machine suspends and resumes, Capsule shall present a frame on the
output within 2 s of resume.

Rationale: suspend never costs the session. Source: CPS-NEED-004. Method:
test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-071 @emph{Hand-back}
When Capsule exits by a path it handles, a panic included, it shall leave
the virtual terminal in text mode accepting keyboard input within 2 s of
exit.

Rationale: no failure strands the seat. Source: CPS-NEED-003. Method: test.
Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-072 @emph{Frame pacing}
Capsule shall submit at most one frame to the output per vertical blank.

Rationale: no tearing and no wasted rendering. Source: CPS-NEED-004.
Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-116 @emph{Seat never active}
When the seat session has not become active within 30 s of being opened,
Capsule shall exit with status 1 after writing an error whose hint names the
virtual terminal it was started on.

Rationale: a session started on a background terminal fails visibly instead
of waiting forever. Source: CPS-NEED-003. Method: test. Priority:
mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-118 @emph{GPU lost}
Capsule shall treat removal of the GPU in use as an error it cannot recover
from (CPS-SRS-117).

Rationale: a vanished GPU leaves nothing to render on. Source:
CPS-NEED-003. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-119 @emph{Seat lost}
Capsule shall treat loss of its seat session as an error it cannot recover
from (CPS-SRS-117).

Rationale: without the seat manager Capsule holds no device it may use.
Source: CPS-NEED-003. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.
@end table

@strong{Assumption A-01.} When Capsule is killed by @code{SIGKILL}, the seat
manager (seatd or systemd-logind) will return the virtual terminal to text
mode, because Capsule can run no code of its own after that signal.  This is
a statement about the platform, not a requirement; it is checked by a system
test on the reference machine at G2 and at every release, and the result is
recorded with the release manifest.

@node Nested backend
@section Nested backend

These requirements apply under the nested backend.

@table @asis
@item CPS-SRS-073 @emph{Window}
Under the nested backend, Capsule shall show its output as one window of the
host session.

Rationale: development and testing need no spare seat. Source:
CPS-NEED-009. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-074 @emph{Window resized}
When the host resizes Capsule's window, Capsule shall resize its output and
reconfigure the fullscreen toplevel to the new size.

Rationale: the program keeps filling the window. Source: CPS-NEED-009.
Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-075 @emph{Window closed}
When the host closes Capsule's window, Capsule shall send @code{SIGTERM} to
the child.

Rationale: closing the window ends the session as a signal would. Source:
CPS-NEED-002. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-076 @emph{No devices}
Under the nested backend, Capsule shall open no DRM or input device node.

Rationale: a nested Capsule never competes with its host for devices.
Source: CPS-NEED-009. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-115 @emph{Host unreachable}
When the nested backend cannot connect to the host display, Capsule shall
exit with status 1 after writing an error whose hint suggests
@option{--backend drm}.

Rationale: a stale @env{WAYLAND_DISPLAY} or @env{DISPLAY} makes the
automatic choice pick the nested backend; the error says how to override it.
Source: CPS-NEED-003; CPS-NEED-007. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.
@end table

@node Keys and accessibility
@section Keys and accessibility

These requirements apply under every backend and mode unless their text
names one.

@table @asis
@item CPS-SRS-077 @emph{No key bindings}
Capsule shall deliver every key combination to the focused client, except
Ctrl+Alt+F1 to F12 under the DRM backend.

Rationale: no screen-reader chord and no program shortcut is ever taken.
Source: CPS-NEED-005; CPS-NEED-008; Standard §10.4. Method: test.
Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-078 @emph{Plain diagnostics}
When @option{--accessible}, @option{--no-color}, or @option{--color never}
is given, or @env{SPACECRAFT_A11Y} or @env{NO_COLOR} is set to a non-empty
value, Capsule shall write no ANSI escape sequence.

Rationale: diagnostics read cleanly through a screen reader and on a dumb
terminal. Source: Standard §18.1; CPS-NEED-008. Method: test. Priority:
mandatory. Status: draft.
@end table

@node Budgets
@section Budgets

These requirements apply under the DRM backend.  Each budget is measured
on the reference machine with the reference workload by a committed script,
run for every release build as part of the release gate (Standard §22.4).
CI runners have no seat or GPU, so CI tracks the nested-backend figures
only, as an early signal.

@table @asis
@item CPS-SRS-079 @emph{Start-up}
Under the DRM backend, Capsule shall start the child within 250 ms of
process start, warm page cache.

Rationale: the greeter hands over without a visible pause. Source:
CPS-NEED-001; Standard §22.1. Method: test. Priority: mandatory.
Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-080 @emph{Memory}
Capsule's peak resident memory over the reference workload shall not exceed
64 MiB.

Rationale: the compositor stays small beside the program it runs. Source:
CPS-NEED-005; Standard §22.1. Method: test. Priority: mandatory.
Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-081 @emph{Input latency}
Capsule shall flush the @code{wl_keyboard.key} event for a key press to the
focused client's socket within 1 ms of libinput delivering that press to
Capsule, at the 99th percentile.

Rationale: typing through Capsule feels like typing without it. Source:
CPS-NEED-001; Standard §22.1. Method: test. Priority: mandatory.
Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-082 @emph{Idle CPU}
Over the 60 s idle phase of the reference workload, Capsule shall use no
more than 60 ms of CPU time.

Rationale: an idle session costs no power. Source: CPS-NEED-004; Standard
§22.1. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-083 @emph{Binary size}
The stripped release binary shall not exceed 12 MiB.

Rationale: the login path stays small. Source: CPS-NEED-005; Standard
§22.1. Method: inspection. Priority: expected. Status: draft.
@end table

@node Release
@section Release

These requirements apply to the repository and the release, whatever the
backend.

@table @asis
@item CPS-SRS-084 @emph{Manual}
@file{doc/capsule.texi} shall build to Info, HTML, and PDF with no error and
no warning.

Rationale: the manual ships in every package. Source: Standard §8. Method:
test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-085 @emph{Nix package}
@file{packaging/default.nix} shall build Capsule at the release version.

Rationale: the first of the three package definitions. Source: Standard
§5.5. Method: test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-086 @emph{Guix package}
@file{packaging/guix.scm} shall build Capsule at the release version.

Rationale: the second package definition. Source: Standard §5.5. Method:
test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-087 @emph{Arch package}
@file{packaging/PKGBUILD} shall build Capsule at the release version.

Rationale: the third package definition. Source: Standard §5.5. Method:
test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-088 @emph{Info directory}
Each of the three package definitions shall install the Info manual with an
@command{install-info} hook.

Rationale: @samp{info capsule} works after installation. Source: Standard
§8. Method: inspection. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-089 @emph{SBOM}
The release CI run shall generate a CycloneDX JSON software bill of
materials from the locked inputs and attach it, with its SHA-256 checksum,
to the GitHub release.

Rationale: every shipped dependency is known. Source: Standard §24.3.
Method: inspection. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-090 @emph{Interface control}
The manual shall carry an @code{Interfaces} node recording, for each
interface, its identity, version, contract, error behaviour, stability
class, and known consumers.

Rationale: the command line and the Wayland surface are changed knowingly.
Source: Standard §23.2. Method: inspection. Priority: mandatory.
Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-091 @emph{Coverage}
The test suite shall reach at least 80% line coverage of Capsule's own
crates.

Rationale: the Category B evidence floor. Source: Standard §21.2. Method:
test. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-092 @emph{Fuzzing}
Each parser shall have a fuzz target that CI runs for at least 60 s on every
merge to @code{main}.

Rationale: generated inputs find what the author did not think of. The
Wayland wire format is parsed by wayland-rs, qualified under §24, not here.
Source: Standard §21.2, §21.4. Method: inspection. Priority: mandatory.
Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-127 @emph{No desktop in the closure}
The runtime closure of Capsule's Nix package shall contain no package of
COSMIC, GNOME, KDE Plasma, or niri.

Rationale: rebuilding a desktop never rebuilds or changes the login path.
Source: CPS-NEED-005. Method: inspection. Priority: mandatory.
Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-093 @emph{Mutation testing}
Each release shall carry a @command{cargo mutants} run whose surviving
mutants are each killed or justified in writing.

Rationale: the tests are shown to notice defects. Source: Standard §21.2.
Method: inspection. Priority: mandatory. Status: draft.
@end table

@node XWayland
@section XWayland

These requirements apply under the DRM and nested backends.

@table @asis
@item CPS-SRS-094 @emph{On demand}
When built with the @code{xwayland} feature and given @option{--xwayland},
Capsule shall start Xwayland when the first X11 client connects.

Rationale: an X11-only program can be the session's program. Source:
CPS-NEED-001. Method: test. Priority: optional. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-095 @emph{Display variable}
When given @option{--xwayland}, Capsule shall add @env{DISPLAY} naming its
Xwayland display to the child's environment.

Rationale: the child finds the X server without configuration. Source:
CPS-NEED-001. Method: test. Priority: optional. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-096 @emph{X11 windows}
Capsule shall apply CPS-SRS-036 to CPS-SRS-039 and CPS-SRS-109 to X11
windows as to Wayland toplevels.

Rationale: an X11 program behaves like a Wayland one. Source: CPS-NEED-001.
Method: test. Priority: optional. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-097 @emph{Off by default}
Without @option{--xwayland}, Capsule shall not start Xwayland.

Rationale: no X server runs unless it is wanted. Source: CPS-NEED-005.
Method: test. Priority: optional. Status: draft.
@end table

@node X11 kiosk mode
@section X11 kiosk mode

These requirements apply in X11 kiosk mode.

@table @asis
@item CPS-SRS-098 @emph{Window manager}
When given @option{--backend x11}, Capsule shall act as the window manager
of the X display named by @env{DISPLAY}.

Rationale: a machine without kernel mode setting gets the same session.
Source: CPS-NEED-010. Method: test. Priority: optional. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-099 @emph{Display taken}
When another window manager already manages that display, Capsule shall exit
with status 5.

Rationale: two window managers never fight over one display. Source: CLI
Standard §4. Method: test. Priority: optional. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-100 @emph{X11 windows fullscreen}
In X11 kiosk mode, Capsule shall map every toplevel that has no
parent at the size of the root window.

Rationale: the program's windows fill the screen, whichever it maps first.
Source: CPS-NEED-010. Method: test. Priority: optional. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-101 @emph{X11 dialogs centred}
In X11 kiosk mode, Capsule shall map every dialog at its requested size,
centred on the root window.

Rationale: dialogs stay visible. Source: CPS-NEED-010. Method: test.
Priority: optional. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-102 @emph{X11 focus}
In X11 kiosk mode, Capsule shall give input focus to the most recently
mapped toplevel.

Rationale: a dialog takes the keys as soon as it appears. Source:
CPS-NEED-010. Method: test. Priority: optional. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-103 @emph{X11 exit status}
In X11 kiosk mode, Capsule shall meet CPS-SRS-014 to CPS-SRS-017.

Rationale: one exit-status contract across modes. Source: CPS-NEED-002.
Method: test. Priority: optional. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-104 @emph{X11 key bindings}
In X11 kiosk mode, Capsule shall grab no key.

Rationale: no screen-reader chord and no program shortcut is taken.
Source: CPS-NEED-008; Standard §10.4. Method: test. Priority: optional.
Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-105 @emph{X11 background}
In X11 kiosk mode, Capsule shall set the root window background to the
canvas colour.

Rationale: the visible background follows the house palette. Source:
Standard §11. Method: test. Priority: optional. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-112 @emph{X11 stacking}
In X11 kiosk mode, Capsule shall stack the most recently mapped toplevel
above every other.

Rationale: a newly opened dialog is never hidden behind the program.
Source: CPS-NEED-010. Method: test. Priority: optional. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-114 @emph{X11 child environment}
In X11 kiosk mode, Capsule shall start the child with Capsule's own
environment unchanged.

Rationale: the program finds the X server through the @env{DISPLAY} it was
started with. Source: CPS-NEED-008; CPS-NEED-010. Method: test. Priority:
optional. Status: draft.

@item CPS-SRS-120 @emph{X display unreachable}
In X11 kiosk mode, when Capsule cannot connect to the display named by
@env{DISPLAY}, it shall exit with status 1 after writing an error whose hint
names the variable.

Rationale: a missing X server is reported, not waited on. Source:
CPS-NEED-007. Method: test. Priority: optional. Status: draft.
@end table

@bye
